-- New-style daily words for 1–31 January (days 1–31): a real devotion (paragraphs ending "Today: …"),
-- a declaration and a prayer. Same KJV verse as before. Only replaces days still in the old one-paragraph style,
-- so anything already rewritten in Admin → Daily words is kept. The words repeat every year.

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah spoke these words to people in exile in Babylon. Their temple was gone, their city was in ruins, and many of them could only look backwards, at what they had lost and at what they had done wrong. God told them to stop staring at the past, because He was about to do something new.

A new year can feel the same way. We arrive carrying last year: the disappointment, the money that ran out, the prayer that seemed unanswered, the mistake we keep replaying. God does not ask us to pretend those things never happened. He asks us not to live there.

Notice the question: "Shall ye not know it?" The new thing is already springing up, but it is possible to miss it if your eyes are fixed behind you. God makes roads in places with no roads, and rivers where there is only sand.

Today: write down one thing from last year you need to stop rehearsing. Hand it to God in prayer, and ask Him to show you the new thing He is doing.',
  declaration = 'I release the former things. God is doing a new thing in my life, and He is making a way for me where I see none.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for a fresh start. I lay down the regrets and disappointments I have been carrying. Open my eyes to see the new thing You are doing, and make a way for me in every dry place this year. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy001' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Lamentations was written after Jerusalem fell. Jeremiah walked through a broken city, and most of the book is grief: honest, raw and painful. Then, right in the middle of the sorrow, he says something surprising: "It is of the LORD''s mercies that we are not consumed."

He had not stopped hurting. The city was still in ruins. But he had remembered something bigger than his pain: God''s compassion had not run out. It was still there every morning, like the sunrise.

That is good news for anyone who woke up tired today, or who failed yesterday. You did not wake up into yesterday''s leftovers. You woke up into fresh mercy, enough for today, from a God whose faithfulness is great.

You do not need to earn this morning''s mercy. It was waiting for you before you opened your eyes.

Today: before you check your phone or start your work, say one sentence of thanks to God for His mercy this morning.',
  declaration = 'The Lord''s mercies are new for me this morning. His compassion has not failed, and great is His faithfulness to me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that Your mercy met me again this morning. Where I failed yesterday, I receive Your forgiveness today. Help me to walk in Your faithfulness and to show the same mercy to others. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy002' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul knew what it meant to have a past. Before he met Jesus he had arrested Christians and watched as Stephen was stoned to death. If anyone could have been defined by his old life, it was Paul. Yet he writes, "If any man be in Christ, he is a new creature."

He does not say "a better version of the old person". He says new. When you belong to Christ, God does not just patch up your old life. He gives you a new identity and a new start.

Many believers still live under old labels: the stubborn child, the one who always fails, the one with that history. People may still use those names. But God has written a new name over your life, and His word is the final one.

Becoming new is a gift you receive at once, and also a journey you walk every day, as old habits give way to new ones.

Today: name one old label you still believe about yourself. Then say out loud what God says instead: "In Christ, I am new."',
  declaration = 'I am in Christ, so I am a new creation. Old things have passed away, and all things have become new in me.',
  prayer = 'Jesus, thank You for making me new. Help me to stop living by the old labels and to walk in who You say I am. Let the newness You have given me show in my thoughts, words and choices today. Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy003' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus said these words in the Sermon on the Mount, right after talking about worry. He had just pointed to the birds that do not plant, and the lilies that do not spin cloth, and reminded His listeners that their Father knows what they need.

"Seek ye first" is about order. Food, clothes, rent and school fees are real needs, and Jesus does not pretend they are not. But He says they cannot be the first thing. When they take first place, they become masters that are never satisfied.

Seeking God''s kingdom first means asking, in every decision, "What does God want here?" before "What do I want?" It means giving Him the first part of your day, your money and your attention, not what is left over.

And then comes the promise: "all these things shall be added unto you." Not because God owes us, but because a Father takes care of children who trust Him.

Today: before you plan your day, give God the first ten minutes. Ask Him what matters most to Him today.',
  declaration = 'I will seek God''s kingdom first. My Father knows what I need, and He will add everything I need to my life.',
  prayer = 'Father, I confess that worry often takes the first place in my heart. Today I choose to seek You first. Order my priorities, provide for my needs, and help me to trust You as a child trusts a good father. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy004' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Habakkuk was frustrated. He looked at the violence and injustice around him and asked God, "How long?" God''s answer was not instant change. It was an instruction: write the vision down, make it plain, and wait for it.

Some of what God has put in your heart will not happen this week. It has "an appointed time". That can be hard, especially when you see others moving ahead. But God''s timing is not slow; it is exact. "It will surely come, it will not tarry."

Writing it down matters. A vision kept only in your head is easy to forget or to doubt. A vision written plainly can be prayed over, planned for and shared. It becomes something you can run with.

Waiting for God is not doing nothing. It is staying faithful while He works, and keeping the vision in front of you so that you are ready when its time comes.

Today: write down one thing you believe God has put in your heart for this year. Keep it somewhere you will see it, and pray over it this week.',
  declaration = 'The vision God has given me will come at its appointed time. I will write it, run with it, and wait for it in faith.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for the dreams You have placed in my heart. Give me clarity to write them plainly, patience to wait for Your timing, and diligence to prepare while I wait. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy005' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David wrote this psalm after knowing both the heaviness of hidden sin and the relief of being forgiven. In the verses before it he describes how his strength dried up while he kept silent, and how joy came back when he confessed. Then God speaks: "I will instruct thee and teach thee in the way which thou shalt go."

God does not only forgive us and leave us to find our own way. He becomes our teacher and our guide. The picture of guiding "with mine eye" is of someone close enough to direct you with a look, the way a parent can guide a child across a room without a word.

That kind of guidance needs closeness. You cannot catch someone''s eye from far away. The more time we spend with God in His Word and in prayer, the more easily we sense His direction.

The next verse warns us not to be like a stubborn horse that must be pulled with a bit. God would rather lead us gently than drag us.

Today: bring one decision you are facing to God in prayer. Ask Him to teach you, and be willing to be led.',
  declaration = 'God is my teacher and my guide. He instructs me in the way I should go, and I will follow His lead.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that You do not leave me to guess my way. Teach me, guide me and keep me close to You, so that I can follow Your direction gently and gladly. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy006' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Isaiah''s day, God''s people had a habit of running to Egypt for help instead of trusting Him. Isaiah warned them, but he also gave them a promise: when you turn back to God, He will guide you so clearly that it will be like hearing a voice behind you saying, "This is the way, walk ye in it."

The promise comes "when ye turn to the right hand, and when ye turn to the left." In other words, when you are at a junction and unsure which way to go, God has not gone silent. His Spirit, His Word and godly counsel help you to know the way.

Notice that the voice is behind you. Often we want to see the whole road ahead before we move. But God''s guidance usually comes one step at a time, as we walk. We hear the word as we go.

The way to hear Him well is to stay close and to keep turning back to Him whenever we drift.

Today: in a quiet moment, ask God, "Is there a way You want me to walk today?" Listen, and take one small step of obedience.',
  declaration = 'God''s Spirit guides me. When I turn to the right or to the left, I will hear His word saying, "This is the way."',
  prayer = 'Holy Spirit, tune my ears to Your voice. When I am unsure, show me the way, and give me courage to walk in it, one step at a time. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy007' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '"Commit thy works unto the LORD" literally carries the idea of rolling something onto Him, like rolling a heavy load off your back and onto someone strong enough to carry it.

Many of us plan first and pray afterwards, asking God to bless what we have already decided. Proverbs turns that around: bring your work to God first, and He will establish your thoughts, settling them and making them firm.

This is not a promise that every plan will work out the way we imagine. Two verses later Proverbs says, "A man''s heart deviseth his way: but the LORD directeth his steps." Committing our works to God means trusting Him with the outcome, and being willing for Him to adjust the plan.

When we do that, something changes inside us. Anxious, scattered thoughts begin to settle. We work hard, but we are no longer carrying the whole weight alone.

Today: before you start your work, roll it onto God in prayer. Name each task and say, "Lord, I commit this to You."',
  declaration = 'I commit my work to the Lord. He establishes my thoughts and directs my steps.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I bring You my work, my plans and my worries today. Settle my thoughts, guide my decisions and let what I do bring You glory. I trust You with the results. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy008' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote these words from prison, near the end of a remarkable life. He had planted churches, written letters and suffered for the gospel, yet he says, "I count not myself to have apprehended." He had not arrived. He was still pressing on.

He describes himself like a runner in a race. A runner who keeps looking back will slow down or stumble. So Paul forgets what is behind, both his failures and his successes, and stretches forward to what is ahead.

Forgetting here does not mean losing your memory. It means not letting the past hold you back. Old guilt can freeze us, and old victories can make us comfortable. Both can stop us from running.

The prize Paul runs towards is "the high calling of God in Christ Jesus": knowing Christ more and becoming like Him. That race is not finished until we see Him.

Today: name one thing from the past, good or bad, that is slowing you down. Ask God for grace to leave it behind and press on.',
  declaration = 'I am forgetting what is behind and pressing towards what is ahead. I will run my race for the prize of God''s calling in Christ.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, I do not want to stand still in my faith. Free me from the weight of yesterday, and give me fresh strength to press on towards knowing You more. Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy009' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 90 is the oldest psalm in the Bible, a prayer of Moses. Moses had watched a whole generation pass away in the wilderness, and he had learned how short a human life is. So he prays, "Teach us to number our days."

Numbering our days does not mean being gloomy about death. It means seeing time as precious, because it is limited. Each day is a gift that will not come back.

When we forget this, we waste days on things that do not matter, put off what does, and assume there will always be time to forgive, to serve, to pray, to call our parents. When we remember it, we begin to live wisely.

Wisdom is not just knowing a lot. It is knowing what matters most and living accordingly. A heart that counts its days carefully learns to spend them on God and on people.

Today: think about how you spent yesterday. Ask God to show you one thing to give more time to, and one thing to give less.',
  declaration = 'My days are a gift from God. I will number them, and I will apply my heart to wisdom.',
  prayer = 'Eternal God, thank You for the gift of today. Teach me to see my time as precious. Help me to spend it on what matters to You, and to waste less of it on what does not. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy010' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Hebrews 11 is sometimes called the hall of faith. It lists men and women who trusted God before they could see the outcome: Noah building an ark under a dry sky, Abraham leaving home without knowing where he was going, Sarah believing for a child long after she was old.

This first verse tells us what faith is. It is "the substance of things hoped for": confidence that what God has promised is real, even before it arrives. It is "the evidence of things not seen": being sure of what our eyes cannot yet show us.

Faith is not pretending, and it is not forcing ourselves to feel positive. Faith rests on someone. It is trusting what God has said because of who God is.

Most of the people in Hebrews 11 did not see everything they hoped for in their lifetime. But they kept trusting, and God counted their faith as precious.

Today: think of one promise of God you are waiting for. Thank Him for it now, before you see it, because He is faithful.',
  declaration = 'My faith rests on God. What He has promised is real to me, even before I see it.',
  prayer = 'Father, strengthen my faith. When I cannot see what You are doing, help me to trust who You are. Let my life, like the saints before me, show that You are faithful. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy011' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '"Delight thyself in the LORD" is not a trick to get what we want. It is an invitation to find our joy in God Himself.

When you truly delight in someone, your desires begin to change. You start to want what they want. As we enjoy God''s presence, read His Word and walk with Him, our hearts are reshaped, and the desires He gives us begin to line up with His will. Then He delights to fulfil them.

The next verse says, "Commit thy way unto the LORD; trust also in him; and he shall bring it to pass." Delight, commit, trust: these three go together. We enjoy God, hand our way to Him, and rest in His timing.

David wrote this psalm when he was old. He had seen wicked people prosper for a time and fade away. His advice is not to fret, but to find our deepest joy in the Lord.

Today: spend five minutes simply enjoying God. Thank Him for who He is, not only for what He gives.',
  declaration = 'I delight myself in the Lord. I commit my way to Him and trust Him, and He brings it to pass.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You are my greatest joy. Shape my desires until they match Yours. I commit my plans to You today, and I trust You to bring to pass what is good. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy012' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David was a busy man: a king with armies, enemies and a nation to lead. Yet he writes, "My voice shalt thou hear in the morning." Before the day''s demands rushed in, he spoke to God.

There is wisdom in giving God the first words of the day. In the morning our minds are not yet crowded. When we pray first, we set the direction of everything that follows.

David says he would "direct" his prayer, a word used for setting things in order, like arranging wood for a sacrifice. Then he would "look up", watching expectantly for God''s answer. Prayer for him was not just talking; it was waiting with hope.

You do not need an hour. Many people in the Bible prayed short, honest prayers. What matters is turning your heart towards God before you turn it towards everything else.

Today: tomorrow morning, before you pick up your phone, pray one honest prayer and give God the first words of your day. Then look up and expect Him to answer.',
  declaration = 'In the morning God hears my voice. I will bring my prayer to Him and look up with expectation.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I give You the first part of my day. Hear my prayer, order my steps, and help me to watch for Your answers with hope. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy013' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'God spoke these words through Ezekiel to a people whose hearts had grown hard. They had turned to idols and ignored His warnings again and again, and it ended in exile. Yet God did not give up on them. He promised something only He could do: a new heart.

A heart of stone does not feel, bend or respond. Many of us know what that is like. After disappointment, hurt or repeated sin, our hearts can grow cold towards God and towards people.

You cannot carve yourself a new heart. Willpower can change some habits, but it cannot make a stone heart soft. This is God''s work: "A new heart also will I give you." Through Jesus and the Holy Spirit, He keeps this promise for everyone who comes to Him.

A heart of flesh is tender. It feels conviction, responds to God''s voice, weeps with those who weep, and loves.

Today: ask God to show you one area where your heart has grown hard, perhaps towards a person, or towards prayer. Invite Him to soften it.',
  declaration = 'God has given me a new heart and a new spirit. My heart is tender towards Him and towards people.',
  prayer = 'Lord, where my heart has grown hard, make it soft again. Take away the stone and give me a heart that loves You and feels for others. Fill me with Your Spirit today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy014' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 119 is the longest chapter in the Bible, and almost every one of its 176 verses is about God''s Word. This verse is one of the most loved: "Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path."

In the psalmist''s day, a lamp was a small clay dish of oil with a wick. It did not light up the whole valley. It gave just enough light for the next few steps on a dark road.

That is often how God''s Word guides us. We may not see where the whole year is going. But as we read and obey Scripture, we see enough for the next step: the next decision, the next conversation, the right thing to do today.

The lamp only helps if you carry it. A Bible on the shelf gives no light. When we read it, remember it and walk by it, we stop stumbling in the dark.

Today: read one short passage of Scripture slowly. Ask God, "What is the next step this shows me?"',
  declaration = 'God''s Word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path. I will walk by its light today.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for Your Word. Light my way through it, one step at a time. Give me a hunger to read it and the courage to obey what I see. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy015' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 58 begins with God correcting His people about their religion. They fasted and prayed loudly, but they oppressed their workers and ignored the poor. God told them that the fast He wants is to loose the bands of wickedness, feed the hungry and care for the afflicted.

Then come the promises, and this is one of them: "The LORD shall guide thee continually, and satisfy thy soul in drought." A life that pours out to others becomes, in God''s hands, like "a watered garden".

There is something beautiful here. We often think that giving will leave us empty. God says the opposite. When we care for others, He keeps refreshing us, even in dry seasons.

The image of "a spring of water, whose waters fail not" is of a source that keeps flowing. Our strength runs out. His does not.

Today: do one practical thing to meet someone else''s need, however small: a meal, a call, a kind word, a gift. Trust God to refresh you as you pour out.',
  declaration = 'The Lord guides me continually and satisfies my soul even in drought. I am a watered garden and a spring that does not fail.',
  prayer = 'Father, guide me today, and make me a blessing to someone in need. When I feel dry, refresh me with Your Spirit, so that Your life keeps flowing through me to others. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy016' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'James wrote to Christians going through trials. Right after telling them to count it all joy when they face hard times, he says something very practical: if you do not know what to do, ask God for wisdom.

Notice how James describes God. He "giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not." In other words, God is generous, and He does not scold you for asking. You do not need to be embarrassed to say, "Lord, I do not know what to do."

Wisdom is more than information. It is knowing how to live well in a real situation: how to handle a difficult person, a money decision, a choice about work or marriage.

James adds one condition: ask in faith, "nothing wavering." Ask expecting God to answer, and be ready to obey when He does. He often answers through Scripture, through a sense of peace, or through godly counsel.

Today: name one situation where you need wisdom. Ask God for it specifically, and expect Him to give generously.',
  declaration = 'When I lack wisdom, I ask God. He gives to me generously and without finding fault.',
  prayer = 'Father, I need Your wisdom today. I bring You the situation that confuses me. Give me Your understanding, and help me to recognise and follow Your answer. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy017' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 16 is a psalm of confidence. David says, "The lines are fallen unto me in pleasant places," and, "I have set the LORD always before me." It ends with this verse: God shows the path of life, and in His presence is fullness of joy.

The world offers many paths to joy: money, success, pleasure, being admired. Some of them give a little happiness for a while, but none of them lasts. David found that real, full joy is found in God''s presence.

"Fulness of joy" means nothing is missing. "Pleasures for evermore" means it does not run out. This is joy that goes deeper than circumstances, the kind of joy believers can have even in sorrow.

Peter quoted this psalm on the day of Pentecost, explaining that it points to Jesus'' resurrection. Because Jesus rose, the path of life is open to us, and His presence is with us now and forever.

Today: take a few quiet minutes to simply be with God. Tell Him that He is your joy, before you ask Him for anything.',
  declaration = 'God shows me the path of life. In His presence I find fullness of joy, and at His right hand are pleasures for evermore.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You are my joy. Lead me on the path of life, and keep me close to You. When I look for joy in the wrong places, draw me back to Your presence. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy018' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus'' words "Ask, seek, knock" come with growing intensity. Asking uses your voice. Seeking uses your effort and attention. Knocking keeps going at a closed door.

In Greek, these words carry the sense of continuing: keep on asking, keep on seeking, keep on knocking. Jesus is not describing a single quick prayer, but a life of persistent, trusting prayer.

He gives the reason in the next verses. If a human father would not give his son a stone when he asks for bread, how much more will our Father in heaven give good things to those who ask Him.

This does not mean God gives us everything exactly as we ask. A good father sometimes says "wait" or "not that". But He never ignores His children, and He always gives what is truly good.

Prayer is not twisting God''s arm. It is coming again and again to a Father who loves to give.

Today: think of one prayer you have stopped praying because the answer seemed slow. Bring it back to God today, and keep knocking.',
  declaration = 'I will keep asking, seeking and knocking, because my Father in heaven gives good things to His children.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that You hear me. Give me persistence in prayer, and trust in Your goodness, so that I keep coming to You whether the answer is yes, wait or something better. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy019' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David wanted many things in his life, but in this psalm he narrows it down to "one thing": to dwell in the house of the Lord, to behold His beauty and to inquire in His temple.

He wrote this while enemies surrounded him. The psalm opens with armies and false witnesses. Yet his deepest desire was not escape or victory. It was to be near God.

That is a powerful way to pray in hard times. Instead of only asking God to change our situation, we ask for more of Him in the middle of it. When God becomes our one thing, our fears begin to shrink.

"To behold the beauty of the LORD" means to gaze at Him, to admire who He is. "To inquire" means to seek His guidance. Worship and listening belong together.

Few of us can say God is our one thing every day. But we can ask Him to make Him so.

Today: if you could ask God for only one thing today, what would it be? Bring it to Him, and then ask Him to be your one thing.',
  declaration = 'One thing I desire of the Lord: to dwell in His presence, to behold His beauty and to seek Him all my days.',
  prayer = 'Lord, in a life full of many desires, I ask for the one thing that matters most: more of You. Draw me into Your presence and let me see Your beauty today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy020' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'God spoke this promise through Isaiah to people who felt lost and blind, unsure of the way ahead. He promised to lead them on paths they did not know, to make darkness light before them, and to straighten crooked places.

There is comfort in the honesty of this verse. It does not say we will always understand the way. It says God will lead us even "by a way that they knew not".

Many of us are walking unfamiliar roads: a new job, a loss, an illness, a move, a season we did not choose. We may feel like blind people stepping into the unknown. But the One who leads us can see perfectly.

The verse ends with a strong promise: "These things will I do unto them, and not forsake them." God does not lead us into the unknown and then leave us there.

Today: name one unfamiliar road you are walking. Tell God honestly that you cannot see the way, and thank Him that He can, and that He will not forsake you.',
  declaration = 'God leads me in paths I have not known. He turns darkness into light before me and makes crooked places straight.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I do not see the whole road, but You do. Lead me through what is unfamiliar, light up the darkness ahead of me, and help me to trust that You will never forsake me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy021' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'This verse is a morning prayer from a man in trouble. In Psalm 143 David says his spirit is overwhelmed and his heart is desolate. Yet he asks God for two things as the day begins: to hear of His lovingkindness, and to know the way he should walk.

Notice the order. Before asking for direction, he asks to be reminded of God''s love. When we are anxious, we want answers first. But the answer we most need is often the reminder that we are loved.

"Cause me to hear thy lovingkindness in the morning" is a beautiful prayer for anyone who wakes up heavy. It asks God to let the first thing we hear be His love, not our fears or our to-do list.

Then David lifts up his soul and asks to be shown the way. God''s love and God''s guidance go together. A loved child is more ready to be led.

Today: when you wake up tomorrow, pray this verse before anything else: "Let me hear Your lovingkindness this morning, and show me the way I should walk."',
  declaration = 'In the morning I hear of God''s lovingkindness. I trust in Him, and He shows me the way I should walk.',
  prayer = 'Father, let Your love be the first thing I hear each morning. Remind me that I am Yours. Then show me the way to walk today, because I lift my soul to You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy022' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Moses spoke these words near the end of his life. He was 120 years old and would not cross the Jordan with the people he had led for forty years. Joshua would take his place, and the people were facing giants and walled cities.

So Moses reminded them, and Joshua especially, of the most important truth: the Lord Himself goes before you. He will be with you. He will not fail you or forsake you. Therefore, "fear not, neither be dismayed."

God does not only walk beside us. He goes ahead of us. Before you arrive at tomorrow''s challenge, He is already there.

Leaders change. People we depend on move away or pass on. Moses could not go with Joshua, but God could. Our security does not rest on any human being, but on the God who never leaves.

Today: think of one situation ahead of you that makes you afraid. Picture the Lord already standing in it before you arrive. Thank Him that He will not fail you.',
  declaration = 'The Lord goes before me and is with me. He will not fail me or forsake me, so I will not fear.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that You go ahead of me into every situation I face. Calm my fears, and give me courage to step forward, knowing You are already there. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy023' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Proverbs 4 is a father teaching his son about two paths: the path of the wicked and the path of the just. This verse describes the path of those who walk with God: it is like the dawn, getting brighter and brighter until the full light of day.

Think of how morning comes. First it is grey, then pale gold, then the sun climbs until everything is bright. Growth in God is often like that: gradual, steady, but real.

Some believers feel discouraged because they are not yet where they want to be in their faith or character. But dawn is not a failure because it is not yet noon. If you are walking with God, your path is getting brighter.

The next verse describes the other path: "The way of the wicked is as darkness: they know not at what they stumble." Walking with God does not mean we never stumble, but it does mean we are walking towards the light.

Today: look back at where you were in your faith a year ago. Thank God for one way your path has grown brighter since then.',
  declaration = 'My path is like the shining light. It grows brighter and brighter as I walk with God.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for the growth You have brought in my life, even when it feels slow. Keep me walking towards Your light, and let my path shine more and more until the perfect day. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy024' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 26 is a song of trust, written for a day when God''s people would sing about His salvation. In the middle of it is this promise: God keeps in perfect peace the one whose mind is stayed on Him.

In Hebrew, "perfect peace" is literally "peace peace", a doubled word meaning complete, full peace. It is not just the absence of trouble. It is a settled wholeness inside, even when trouble is outside.

The key is where the mind is "stayed", meaning resting or leaning. Our minds are always leaning on something. Often they lean on our worries, replaying them over and over. Isaiah says to lean your mind on God instead.

That takes practice. Every time anxious thoughts come, we can turn them into prayer and bring our attention back to who God is and what He has promised.

The reason is simple: "because he trusteth in thee." Peace grows where trust grows.

Today: when a worried thought comes, pause and say, "Lord, I trust You with this." Do it every time it returns.',
  declaration = 'God keeps me in perfect peace, because my mind is stayed on Him and I trust in Him.',
  prayer = 'Lord, my mind runs to my worries so easily. Teach me to lean my thoughts on You instead. Keep me in Your perfect peace today, because I choose to trust You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy025' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Micah wrote in a dark time. Corruption was everywhere; even families were turning on each other. But in the last chapter of his book, he speaks with defiant hope: "Rejoice not against me, O mine enemy: when I fall, I shall arise."

Notice that he does not say "if I fall". He says "when". Falling is part of life, even for those who love God. We fail, we suffer loss, we sit in dark seasons.

But falling is not the end of the story. "I shall arise." And even while sitting in darkness, "the LORD shall be a light unto me."

This is not pride. Micah''s confidence is not in himself but in God. A few verses later he praises God who "delighteth in mercy" and casts our sins into the depths of the sea.

If you have fallen, the enemy may be whispering that you are finished. God''s word says otherwise.

Today: if there is an area where you have fallen, confess it honestly to God, receive His mercy, and say aloud, "I shall arise."',
  declaration = 'When I fall, I shall arise. When I sit in darkness, the Lord is my light.',
  prayer = 'Merciful God, thank You that my falls are not the end of my story. Lift me up where I have fallen, shine Your light into my darkness, and help me to rise again in Your strength. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy026' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David wrote Psalm 63 "in the wilderness of Judah", probably while running from danger. He was in a literal desert, dry and thirsty. And he used that thirst to describe his longing for God: "My soul thirsteth for thee... in a dry and thirsty land, where no water is."

Spiritual thirst is not a bad sign. It is evidence that we were made for God. When we feel empty, the problem is not that we feel it, but that we often try to fill it with things that cannot satisfy.

"Early will I seek thee" means earnestly, and also at the beginning of the day. David did not wait until the dry season was over to seek God. He sought Him right in the middle of it.

Later in the psalm he says, "My soul shall be satisfied as with marrow and fatness." The thirst turns into satisfaction when we bring it to the right source.

Today: if you feel dry, do not hide it. Tell God, "I am thirsty for You." Then spend a few minutes in a psalm, letting Him meet you there.',
  declaration = 'God is my God. I seek Him earnestly, and in Him my thirsty soul is satisfied.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I am thirsty for You. In dry seasons, draw me closer instead of letting me drift away. Satisfy my soul with Your presence, because Your love is better than life. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy027' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 118 was sung by the people of Israel at their great feasts, and it is likely the hymn Jesus sang with His disciples after the Last Supper, the night before He died. That gives this verse a deeper meaning.

Just before it, the psalm says, "The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner." Jesus applied that to Himself. The day the Lord has made is ultimately the day of salvation, when the rejected stone became the cornerstone.

So when we say, "This is the day which the LORD hath made," we are not only saying that God made today''s sunrise. We are saying that He is working out His saving purposes, even on days that do not look good.

Jesus could sing this the night before the cross. We can choose to rejoice today, not because everything is easy, but because God is at work.

Today: say this verse out loud as you start your day. Then name three things you can thank God for, right now, in this day He has made.',
  declaration = 'This is the day the Lord has made. I will rejoice and be glad in it.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for this day. You made it, and You are working in it. Help me to rejoice in You, whatever it brings, and to look for Your hand at work. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy028' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Hebrews 11 tells the story of Enoch, a man who "walked with God", and then explains what pleased God about him: faith. "Without faith it is impossible to please him."

This verse tells us what that faith includes. First, believing "that he is": that God is real and present, not a distant idea. Second, believing "that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him."

The second part surprises some people. God is pleased when we expect Him to respond to our seeking. He is not reluctant, and He does not hide from those who truly look for Him.

The reward is, above all, God Himself: knowing Him, walking with Him, being changed by Him. Many other blessings flow from that, but He is the treasure.

Diligent seeking means returning to Him consistently, not just in emergencies. It means prayer, Scripture and obedience as a way of life.

Today: when you pray, begin by saying, "Lord, I believe that You are here, and that You reward those who seek You." Then seek Him with expectation.',
  declaration = 'I believe that God is, and that He rewards those who diligently seek Him. I will seek Him with my whole heart.',
  prayer = 'Father, I believe You are real and near. Strengthen my faith, and give me a heart that seeks You diligently. Let my life be one that pleases You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy029' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'This psalm is an acrostic: each verse begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet, like a carefully crafted prayer. In these verses David asks God to show him, teach him and lead him.

Notice that he asks for God''s ways and paths, not just answers. He wants to learn how God thinks, not only what to do next. That is the prayer of someone who wants to grow, not just to be rescued.

He also says, "On thee do I wait all the day." Learning from God is not a quick lesson. It is a daily posture of waiting, listening and following.

Later in the psalm David confesses his sins and asks God not to remember the sins of his youth. God''s guidance is for imperfect people. We do not have to be perfect before we can be taught.

The psalm also promises that God "will teach sinners in the way" and "the meek will he guide". Humility opens the door to being led.

Today: pray this verse slowly, word by word. Then ask God to teach you one thing about His ways today.',
  declaration = 'The Lord shows me His ways and teaches me His paths. He is the God of my salvation, and I wait on Him all day.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I want to know Your ways, not just my next step. Teach me, lead me in Your truth, and make me humble and teachable. I wait on You today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy030' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '"The steps of a good man are ordered by the LORD." That is a comforting truth: God is involved in the details of the lives of those who walk with Him. Not just the big decisions, but the steps.

But verse 24 is just as comforting: "Though he fall, he shall not be utterly cast down: for the LORD upholdeth him with his hand."

David does not say that good people never fall. He says that when they do, God''s hand is holding them. They may stumble, but they will not be destroyed.

Think of a child learning to walk while holding a parent''s hand. The child still trips, but does not hit the ground, because the parent''s grip is stronger than the child''s.

This psalm was written by David in his old age. He had seen many falls, including his own. And he testifies that God had never let go.

Today: thank God for one time He caught you when you fell. Then trust Him with your steps today, knowing His hand is holding you.',
  declaration = 'The Lord orders my steps and delights in my way. Even if I fall, I will not be utterly cast down, because He holds me by His hand.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for ordering my steps and for holding my hand. When I stumble, catch me and lift me up. Help me to walk today with confidence in Your care. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy031' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;
